{# Every section is a {% block %}, so --prompts reaches all of them; those stating how the server behaves are safest added to rather than replaced. #}
{% block intro %}Panel Live Server executes Python code snippets and renders the resulting visualizations as live, interactive web pages.{% endblock %}

{% block workflow %}WORKFLOW:
`show(code, name, method)` renders a visualization and gives it to the user.
`screenshot(code=...)` renders one and returns the picture to YOU only — nothing
reaches the chat and nothing is added to the user's feed.
So when you want to check your own output before delivering it, screenshot the
draft, fix what is wrong, screenshot again, and call `show` once at the end.
Never call `show` on work you are still iterating on, and never call it just to
get a snippet_id to screenshot.
Static validation (syntax, security, packages) runs in ~50 ms.
The iframe loads immediately via Panel's WebSocket — no prior validate() needed.{% endblock %}

{% block file_policy %}DO NOT write the visualization code to a file, script, notebook, or `examples/`
directory, and do not run it in a separate shell/REPL. Pass the code string
straight to `show(code=...)` — it executes the code for you. Creating files
clutters the user's repository and is not wanted.{% endblock %}

{% block library_selection %}LIBRARY SELECTION:
PRIMARILY write visualizations with HoloViz packages — they integrate best with
the live server and are ALWAYS installed:
- hvPlot: quick interactive plots from DataFrames (.hvplot API)
- HoloViews: advanced composable, interactive visualizations
- Panel: dashboards, data apps, complex layouts (use method='server')
Also always available (no extra package needed):
- Bokeh: HoloViz's default backend, for low-level interactive web plots
- ECharts (pn.pane.ECharts): business-quality charts with transitions/animations, from a spec dict
- deck.gl (pn.pane.DeckGL): large-scale geospatial and 3D visualization, from a spec dict
Other well-known libraries (Matplotlib, Plotly, seaborn, Altair) are installed ONLY
when the optional 'pydata' dependencies are present, so they may be MISSING — prefer
HoloViz. You do NOT need to check availability up front: if an import is not installed,
`show` returns the validation error — read it and rewrite using a HoloViz package.{% endblock %}

{% block rendering %}RENDERING
Visualizations are served by the live Panel server, so Panel reactive patterns (pn.bind, @pn.depends, param.watch, .servable()) work everywhere. Use method='server' for any interactive Panel app.
Clients whose iframes cannot reach localhost (Claude Desktop, Cowork) show an 'Open in browser' button instead of an inline preview; the visualization itself is fully interactive once opened. Nothing about the code you write changes.{% endblock %}

{% block output %}OUTPUT
After calling `show`, ALWAYS present the returned URL to the user as a clickable Markdown link: [Show Visualization](url){% endblock %}

{% block errors %}ERRORS
`show` raises `SecurityError` for blocked imports or dangerous patterns — these require a substantive code rewrite, not a retry. `show` raises `ValidationError` for syntax errors, missing packages, or missing Panel extension declarations — fix the reported issue and try again.{% endblock %}
